1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:05,091
On SAT table and graph questions, a lot
of wrong answers aren't wrong about the

2
00:00:05,111 --> 00:00:05,627
data.

3
00:00:05,647 --> 00:00:07,555
They read the table correctly.

4
00:00:07,575 --> 00:00:10,504
They just don't prove the
point the sentence is making.

5
00:00:10,565 --> 00:00:11,331
That's the trap.

6
00:00:11,351 --> 00:00:14,688
And it's why the first thing you read
on these questions isn't the table.

7
00:00:14,708 --> 00:00:15,839
It's the claim.

8
00:00:15,859 --> 00:00:18,446
In this video, you'll
get a three-step method,

9
00:00:18,466 --> 00:00:23,232
six original SAT-style questions
with tables and graphs,

10
00:00:23,252 --> 00:00:27,502
and the four traps that show up on
these questions again and again.

11
00:00:27,536 --> 00:00:30,159
The College Board calls this
skill Command of Evidence,

12
00:00:30,179 --> 00:00:31,194
quantitative.

13
00:00:31,214 --> 00:00:33,587
It's part of the Information
and Ideas domain,

14
00:00:33,607 --> 00:00:37,250
which is about a quarter of the
Reading and Writing section.

15
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:39,634
You'll see a short passage and a table,

16
00:00:39,654 --> 00:00:41,562
a bar graph, or a line graph.

17
00:00:41,582 --> 00:00:44,241
The passage ends with a blank or a claim,

18
00:00:44,261 --> 00:00:46,419
and the question asks something like this:

19
00:00:46,439 --> 00:00:50,670
which choice most effectively uses
data from the table to complete the

20
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:51,428
statement?

21
00:00:51,448 --> 00:00:54,071
Some versions ask you
to complete an example,

22
00:00:54,091 --> 00:00:56,269
or to support someone's conclusion.

23
00:00:56,341 --> 00:00:58,570
The job is the same every time.

24
00:00:58,590 --> 00:01:01,070
The right answer has to pass two tests.

25
00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:02,768
Test one: is it accurate?

26
00:01:02,876 --> 00:01:06,518
Every number and every comparison
has to match the graphic.

27
00:01:06,590 --> 00:01:08,319
Test two: is it relevant?

28
00:01:08,339 --> 00:01:11,641
It has to show exactly
what the sentence claims.

29
00:01:11,661 --> 00:01:15,983
Many wrong answers pass
test one and fail test two.

30
00:01:16,117 --> 00:01:17,117
Here's the method.

31
00:01:17,153 --> 00:01:19,331
Step one: find the claim.

32
00:01:19,474 --> 00:01:23,169
Read the sentence with the blank
and pin down exactly what it needs.

33
00:01:23,189 --> 00:01:24,026
Which group?

34
00:01:24,046 --> 00:01:25,062
Which year?

35
00:01:25,082 --> 00:01:27,545
More, less, or a change over time?

36
00:01:27,565 --> 00:01:30,367
Step two: find exactly those data.

37
00:01:30,387 --> 00:01:34,188
Go to the one row, column, bar,
or point the claim is about.

38
00:01:34,208 --> 00:01:36,136
You can ignore the rest of the graphic.

39
00:01:36,216 --> 00:01:38,874
Step three: check every
number in the choice.

40
00:01:38,894 --> 00:01:41,803
Right row, right column, right direction.

41
00:01:41,823 --> 00:01:44,410
If one number or one comparison is off,

42
00:01:44,430 --> 00:01:46,002
the whole choice is wrong.

43
00:01:46,050 --> 00:01:47,050
Question one.

44
00:01:47,086 --> 00:01:51,208
A school librarian recorded the number
of print books and e-books that students

45
00:01:51,228 --> 00:01:54,208
in each grade checked
out during one semester.

46
00:01:54,228 --> 00:01:57,387
Ninth graders checked out
more print books than e-books,

47
00:01:57,407 --> 00:02:00,192
but twelfth graders showed
the opposite pattern:

48
00:02:00,371 --> 00:02:01,128
blank

49
00:02:01,148 --> 00:02:04,520
Which choice most effectively uses
data from the table to complete the

50
00:02:04,540 --> 00:02:05,301
statement?

51
00:02:05,321 --> 00:02:06,571
Pause and try it.

52
00:02:09,805 --> 00:02:11,571
Step one: find the claim.

53
00:02:11,591 --> 00:02:15,805
The blank is about twelfth graders,
and it says opposite pattern.

54
00:02:15,912 --> 00:02:18,233
Ninth graders checked
out more print books.

55
00:02:18,269 --> 00:02:22,162
So twelfth graders must have checked
out more e-books than print books.

56
00:02:22,228 --> 00:02:24,422
Step two: go to the twelfth grade row.

57
00:02:24,442 --> 00:02:28,156
Two hundred ten print books,
three hundred fifty-six e-books.

58
00:02:28,299 --> 00:02:29,065
More e-books.

59
00:02:29,085 --> 00:02:31,851
Step three: check choice A.

60
00:02:31,871 --> 00:02:34,601
Twelfth graders, three
hundred fifty-six e-books,

61
00:02:34,621 --> 00:02:36,422
two hundred ten print books.

62
00:02:36,442 --> 00:02:37,708
Every number matches.

63
00:02:37,728 --> 00:02:39,478
The answer is A.

64
00:02:39,511 --> 00:02:40,526
Now the wrong ones.

65
00:02:40,546 --> 00:02:42,883
C is the right row, but
it swaps the columns.

66
00:02:42,903 --> 00:02:44,812
D is accurate, but it's eleventh grade.

67
00:02:44,832 --> 00:02:48,081
That's trap number one:
the wrong row or column.

68
00:02:48,296 --> 00:02:50,883
And B is accurate too, but
it's about ninth graders.

69
00:02:50,903 --> 00:02:52,741
That's the first half of the sentence,

70
00:02:52,761 --> 00:02:53,788
not the blank.

71
00:02:53,808 --> 00:02:54,915
Question two.

72
00:02:55,058 --> 00:03:00,181
Students in a psychology class asked
volunteers to study a list of thirty words

73
00:03:00,201 --> 00:03:03,609
in one of three conditions: in silence,

74
00:03:03,629 --> 00:03:07,297
with instrumental music, or
with music that had lyrics.

75
00:03:07,317 --> 00:03:12,583
The students concluded that music with
lyrics interfered with recall more than

76
00:03:12,603 --> 00:03:14,067
instrumental music did.

77
00:03:14,149 --> 00:03:18,093
Which choice most effectively uses data
from the graph to support the students'

78
00:03:18,113 --> 00:03:19,042
conclusion?

79
00:03:19,062 --> 00:03:20,312
Pause and try it.

80
00:03:24,546 --> 00:03:26,240
Here's what makes this one tricky.

81
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:27,882
All four choices are true.

82
00:03:27,902 --> 00:03:29,848
Every one of them matches the graph.

83
00:03:29,868 --> 00:03:31,740
So test one won't help you here.

84
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:33,260
Only test two will.

85
00:03:33,290 --> 00:03:34,755
So go back to the claim.

86
00:03:34,933 --> 00:03:38,520
Lyrics interfered more
than instrumental music.

87
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:41,199
That's a comparison
between two specific bars:

88
00:03:41,219 --> 00:03:43,039
lyrics and instrumental.

89
00:03:43,111 --> 00:03:45,575
Silence isn't part of the claim at all.

90
00:03:45,598 --> 00:03:48,311
Choice B compares exactly those two bars:

91
00:03:48,383 --> 00:03:50,863
thirteen point two words with lyrics,

92
00:03:50,883 --> 00:03:54,062
against seventeen point nine
with instrumental music.

93
00:03:54,098 --> 00:03:56,168
Lower recall with lyrics.

94
00:03:56,311 --> 00:03:57,986
The answer is B.

95
00:03:58,006 --> 00:04:00,450
A and D compare music to silence.

96
00:04:00,470 --> 00:04:03,078
C compares instrumental music to silence.

97
00:04:03,221 --> 00:04:05,772
All true, all about the wrong bars.

98
00:04:05,792 --> 00:04:08,756
That's trap number two:
true but irrelevant.

99
00:04:09,005 --> 00:04:11,255
Watch for it on every data question.

100
00:04:11,289 --> 00:04:12,305
Question three.

101
00:04:12,325 --> 00:04:17,091
A city's transit office tracked the
average number of weekday riders on two bus

102
00:04:17,111 --> 00:04:20,111
routes from twenty nineteen
to twenty twenty-four.

103
00:04:20,539 --> 00:04:24,626
In twenty nineteen, Route Four
was the busier of the two routes.

104
00:04:24,646 --> 00:04:27,754
By twenty twenty-four,
however, that had changed:

105
00:04:27,959 --> 00:04:28,689
blank

106
00:04:28,709 --> 00:04:32,546
Same kind of question: which choice
uses data from the graph to complete the

107
00:04:32,566 --> 00:04:33,331
statement?

108
00:04:33,351 --> 00:04:34,601
Pause and try it.

109
00:04:38,835 --> 00:04:41,943
The claim: by twenty
twenty-four, things had changed.

110
00:04:42,227 --> 00:04:44,208
Route Four was busier in twenty nineteen,

111
00:04:44,228 --> 00:04:46,494
so now Route Nine has to be busier.

112
00:04:46,514 --> 00:04:47,565
Go to twenty twenty-four.

113
00:04:47,585 --> 00:04:50,173
Route Nine is at about fifty-six hundred.

114
00:04:50,193 --> 00:04:52,443
Route Four is at about forty-five hundred.

115
00:04:52,533 --> 00:04:57,727
So Route Four had about eleven
hundred fewer riders than Route Nine.

116
00:04:57,747 --> 00:04:59,033
That's choice A.

117
00:04:59,053 --> 00:04:59,426
Now look at B.

118
00:04:59,446 --> 00:05:01,639
Same number, same year.

119
00:05:01,659 --> 00:05:05,926
But it says Route Nine had eleven
hundred fewer riders than Route Four.

120
00:05:05,946 --> 00:05:07,068
That's backward.

121
00:05:07,088 --> 00:05:10,569
Trap number three: a comparison
in the wrong direction.

122
00:05:10,589 --> 00:05:12,676
When two choices share the same numbers,

123
00:05:12,696 --> 00:05:14,696
check which way the comparison runs.

124
00:05:14,752 --> 00:05:18,268
C is true, but it compares
each route to twenty twenty,

125
00:05:18,288 --> 00:05:19,339
not to each other.

126
00:05:19,359 --> 00:05:22,589
And D gives Route Nine's
number without Route Four's,

127
00:05:22,609 --> 00:05:25,217
so it can't show which route was busier.

128
00:05:25,264 --> 00:05:26,514
Question four.

129
00:05:26,657 --> 00:05:30,621
A team of foresters planted
tree seedlings at four sites.

130
00:05:30,728 --> 00:05:34,922
At each site, half of the seedlings were
watered once a week during their first

131
00:05:34,942 --> 00:05:37,601
summer, and the other
half were not watered.

132
00:05:37,621 --> 00:05:41,708
The foresters concluded that weekly
watering improved survival at most,

133
00:05:41,728 --> 00:05:43,692
but not all, of the sites.

134
00:05:43,717 --> 00:05:47,911
Which choice most effectively uses data
from the table to support the foresters'

135
00:05:47,931 --> 00:05:48,882
conclusion?

136
00:05:48,902 --> 00:05:50,152
Pause and try it.

137
00:05:54,386 --> 00:05:55,795
The claim has two parts.

138
00:05:55,815 --> 00:05:58,081
Watering helped at most sites.

139
00:05:58,101 --> 00:05:59,902
And it didn't help at all of them.

140
00:05:59,922 --> 00:06:04,330
So the right answer needs both: the
sites where watered seedlings did better,

141
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:06,422
and a site where they didn't.

142
00:06:06,478 --> 00:06:07,763
Check each row.

143
00:06:07,942 --> 00:06:10,493
North: eighty-two beats sixty-four.

144
00:06:10,513 --> 00:06:13,707
East: seventy-five beats seventy.

145
00:06:13,727 --> 00:06:15,978
South: ninety beats fifty-eight.

146
00:06:16,228 --> 00:06:19,013
West: sixty-eight is
less than seventy-one.

147
00:06:19,085 --> 00:06:21,458
Three sites yes, one site no.

148
00:06:21,478 --> 00:06:23,406
Choice C says exactly that.

149
00:06:23,430 --> 00:06:26,696
A says watered seedlings
did better at every site.

150
00:06:26,716 --> 00:06:28,410
The West row says otherwise.

151
00:06:28,430 --> 00:06:31,430
That's trap number four:
overgeneralization.

152
00:06:31,679 --> 00:06:36,315
Words like every, all, and always
need every single row to agree.

153
00:06:36,335 --> 00:06:40,136
B is true, but it's one number
with nothing to compare it to.

154
00:06:40,156 --> 00:06:42,764
And D gives only the West site.

155
00:06:42,871 --> 00:06:46,421
It shows the not all part,
but not the most part.

156
00:06:46,441 --> 00:06:48,390
Half of a claim isn't enough.

157
00:06:48,410 --> 00:06:49,854
So those are the four traps.

158
00:06:49,874 --> 00:06:51,533
Wrong row or column.

159
00:06:51,553 --> 00:06:53,068
True but irrelevant.

160
00:06:53,088 --> 00:06:54,175
Wrong direction.

161
00:06:54,195 --> 00:06:55,889
And overgeneralization.

162
00:06:55,909 --> 00:06:58,140
On harder questions, they don't go away.

163
00:06:58,160 --> 00:06:59,497
They just get harder to see.

164
00:06:59,517 --> 00:07:00,470
Here are two.

165
00:07:00,490 --> 00:07:01,577
Question five.

166
00:07:01,597 --> 00:07:05,184
Researchers surveyed all the volunteers
at three nature centers about whether

167
00:07:05,204 --> 00:07:08,791
they had ever submitted a bird
sighting to an online database.

168
00:07:08,811 --> 00:07:11,149
Center Two had the fewest volunteers,

169
00:07:11,169 --> 00:07:14,812
but the researchers found that it had
the highest rate of participation:

170
00:07:15,017 --> 00:07:15,748
blank

171
00:07:15,768 --> 00:07:17,018
Pause and try it.

172
00:07:21,252 --> 00:07:22,018
Find the claim.

173
00:07:22,038 --> 00:07:25,661
It's not about the most volunteers
who submitted a sighting.

174
00:07:25,681 --> 00:07:27,161
It's about the highest rate.

175
00:07:27,181 --> 00:07:28,787
A rate is a percent.

176
00:07:28,894 --> 00:07:32,157
So the only column that
matters is the last one.

177
00:07:32,177 --> 00:07:34,227
Center Two: sixty percent.

178
00:07:34,247 --> 00:07:35,692
Center One: forty.

179
00:07:35,712 --> 00:07:37,442
Center Three: thirty-five.

180
00:07:37,462 --> 00:07:41,391
Choice D gives all three, and
Center Two is the highest.

181
00:07:41,533 --> 00:07:42,569
That's the answer.

182
00:07:42,619 --> 00:07:43,670
The trap is A.

183
00:07:43,690 --> 00:07:48,134
Center Three had one hundred five
volunteers who submitted a sighting,

184
00:07:48,154 --> 00:07:50,404
the biggest number in that column.

185
00:07:50,476 --> 00:07:53,226
True, but that's a count, not a rate.

186
00:07:53,475 --> 00:07:54,511
Wrong column.

187
00:07:54,619 --> 00:07:58,027
B only shows that Center
Two was the smallest.

188
00:07:58,047 --> 00:08:00,756
And C leaves Center Two out completely.

189
00:08:00,776 --> 00:08:03,097
Last one, and it's the hardest.

190
00:08:03,275 --> 00:08:07,898
Researchers compared the average July
surface temperatures of three lakes in

191
00:08:07,918 --> 00:08:10,383
nineteen ninety-five and twenty twenty.

192
00:08:10,419 --> 00:08:13,149
All three lakes warmed,
but not by the same amount.

193
00:08:13,169 --> 00:08:16,792
In fact, the lake that warmed the most
was the one that had been the coolest in

194
00:08:16,812 --> 00:08:19,250
nineteen ninety-five: blank

195
00:08:19,302 --> 00:08:20,675
Pause and try it.

196
00:08:20,695 --> 00:08:22,231
Take a little longer on this one.

197
00:08:26,483 --> 00:08:27,427
Find the claim.

198
00:08:27,447 --> 00:08:28,713
It has two parts.

199
00:08:28,733 --> 00:08:31,840
Which lake was the coolest
in nineteen ninety-five?

200
00:08:32,162 --> 00:08:34,483
And which lake warmed the most?

201
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:36,534
Warmed the most is a change.

202
00:08:36,554 --> 00:08:38,320
So you're not looking for the tallest bar.

203
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:40,947
You're looking for the
biggest gap between bars.

204
00:08:41,036 --> 00:08:44,194
The coolest in nineteen
ninety-five was Lake Q,

205
00:08:44,214 --> 00:08:47,107
at eighteen point five degrees Celsius.

206
00:08:47,321 --> 00:08:48,571
Now the changes.

207
00:08:48,678 --> 00:08:50,659
Lake P went up one degree.

208
00:08:50,679 --> 00:08:52,551
Lake Q went up two and a half.

209
00:08:52,571 --> 00:08:54,551
Lake R went up half a degree.

210
00:08:54,571 --> 00:08:58,159
Lake Q warmed the most, and
choice C says exactly that,

211
00:08:58,179 --> 00:08:59,750
with every number right.

212
00:08:59,801 --> 00:09:00,710
The trap is A.

213
00:09:00,730 --> 00:09:03,817
Lake R has the tallest bars in both years.

214
00:09:03,837 --> 00:09:05,623
It's the warmest lake.

215
00:09:05,658 --> 00:09:08,730
But the warmest lake isn't
the lake that warmed the most.

216
00:09:09,157 --> 00:09:12,174
That's true but irrelevant
in its hardest form:

217
00:09:12,194 --> 00:09:15,300
the highest value instead
of the biggest change.

218
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:18,320
B is true, but it leaves out Lake Q.

219
00:09:18,427 --> 00:09:20,570
And D gets half the claim.

220
00:09:20,999 --> 00:09:23,979
Lake Q was still the
coolest in twenty twenty.

221
00:09:23,999 --> 00:09:27,499
But D never says that
Lake Q warmed the most.

222
00:09:27,572 --> 00:09:30,337
Here's the whole method in one place.

223
00:09:30,357 --> 00:09:34,194
First, read the sentence with the
blank before you study the graphic.

224
00:09:34,214 --> 00:09:37,016
Mark exactly what it needs: which group,

225
00:09:37,036 --> 00:09:39,714
which time, and what kind of comparison.

226
00:09:39,739 --> 00:09:42,505
Second, go only to those data.

227
00:09:42,525 --> 00:09:45,719
One row, one column, one pair of bars.

228
00:09:45,739 --> 00:09:48,647
If the claim says rate, use the percent.

229
00:09:48,667 --> 00:09:50,811
If it says change, subtract.

230
00:09:50,844 --> 00:09:55,289
Third, check every number and every
comparison word in the choice.

231
00:09:55,309 --> 00:09:56,539
More or fewer.

232
00:09:56,559 --> 00:09:57,789
Higher or lower.

233
00:09:57,809 --> 00:09:59,809
Every, most, or some.

234
00:09:59,915 --> 00:10:03,987
Then ask one last question: does
this choice prove the claim,

235
00:10:04,236 --> 00:10:05,587
or is it just true?

236
00:10:05,607 --> 00:10:06,643
One more tip.

237
00:10:06,679 --> 00:10:10,480
Don't try to understand the whole
graphic before you read the claim.

238
00:10:10,500 --> 00:10:13,622
Most of the data on the screen
won't matter for the claim,

239
00:10:13,642 --> 00:10:17,564
and that extra data is exactly
what the wrong answers like to use.

240
00:10:17,584 --> 00:10:18,278
Recap.

241
00:10:18,298 --> 00:10:19,778
Read the claim first.

242
00:10:19,798 --> 00:10:21,707
Find exactly those data.

243
00:10:21,727 --> 00:10:23,064
Check every number.

244
00:10:23,084 --> 00:10:26,777
And watch for the four
traps: wrong row or column,

245
00:10:26,797 --> 00:10:29,670
true but irrelevant, wrong direction,

246
00:10:29,690 --> 00:10:31,548
and overgeneralization.

247
00:10:31,606 --> 00:10:35,427
All six questions in this video
were written for this video,

248
00:10:35,463 --> 00:10:39,122
in the style of the real test,
and the data are made up.

249
00:10:39,142 --> 00:10:43,478
For official practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter by

250
00:10:43,498 --> 00:10:46,106
skill, including Command of Evidence.

251
00:10:46,143 --> 00:10:47,857
If this helped, subscribe.

252
00:10:47,964 --> 00:10:51,607
The next video covers another
skill from this same domain:

253
00:10:51,679 --> 00:10:55,980
inference questions, the ones that ask
which choice most logically completes the

254
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:57,000
text.
